---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import OnDutyCalculator from '../components/OnDutyCalculator.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Why is there no statewide CAL FIRE calendar to pick from?',
    a: 'Unlike Houston or LA County, CAL FIRE schedules are set at the unit and station level — there is no single statewide platoon rotation to anchor to. That is why this page asks for the first day of one of your own 72-hour tours instead of a platoon letter.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the CAL FIRE 66-hour workweek transition?',
    a: 'Under the 2022 agreement with CAL FIRE Local 2881, the department began moving from the long-standing 72-hour average workweek to 66 hours starting November 1, 2024, phased over several years as thousands of new positions are filled. Mechanically it works out to roughly one fewer 24-hour shift per 28-day work period; the basic 3-on/4-off skeleton remains, so this calculator stays valid — just expect an occasional extra scheduled day off as your unit phases in.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is CAL FIRE overtime structured?',
    a: 'Planned overtime is built into the schedule: hours beyond the FLSA threshold for the 28-day work period are paid as overtime, which historically meant roughly 19 OT hours in a typical week under the 72-hour model. During fire season, actual hours can far exceed the base pattern through holdovers and assignments.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this work for seasonal firefighters?',
    a: 'Yes for your base schedule — anchor on any first duty day of a tour. But peak-season staffing, off-forest assignments, and station coverage moves override the base pattern in practice, so treat the calendar as your default rhythm rather than a guarantee in August.',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  title="CAL FIRE Shift Schedule Calendar — 72-Hour Week, 3 On 4 Off"
  description="Build your CAL FIRE duty calendar: enter the first day of one 3-day (72-hour) tour and see your full year of duty days and 4-day breaks, holidays answered, exportable to your phone."
  h1="CAL FIRE Shift Calendar"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    CAL FIRE's base rhythm is famous and grueling: <strong>three consecutive 24-hour duty days,
    then four days off</strong> — the "72-hour workweek" that the department is gradually easing
    toward 66 hours. Because schedules anchor at the unit level, enter the first day of any tour
    you know and this page projects your year: every duty block, every four-day break, every
    holiday answered.
  </p>

  <OnDutyCalculator defaultPattern="cal-fire" />

  <h2>The 3-on/4-off week</h2>
  <p>
    The pattern's virtue is its simplicity: a seven-day cycle means your duty days hold the same
    weekdays for as long as the anchor holds — a crew that starts tours on Mondays keeps
    Monday-through-Wednesday duty and a Thursday-to-Sunday break, week after week. That makes CAL
    FIRE one of the few 24-hour schedules where "which days do I work" has a stable answer, and it
    is why the four-day break feels like a standing long weekend. The cost is the tour itself:
    72 continuous hours on duty, with sleep whenever the incident load allows.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 2024–2029 transition to a 66-hour average changes the arithmetic, not the shape: roughly
    one additional day off per 28-day period as staffing grows. Your unit's implementation decides
    where those extra days land — when yours phases in, keep using this calendar for the base
    pattern and overlay the scheduled relief days your captain posts.
  </p>

  <h2>Holidays, fire season, and reality</h2>
  <p>
    With a stable weekly anchor, holiday luck is decided once: if your tours sit
    Monday–Wednesday, you own every Thursday holiday for the life of the anchor — check the list
    above and you know your Thanksgivings for years, not months. The big caveat is season: base
    schedules bend during major incidents, and every CAL FIRE family knows August plans are written
    in pencil. Use the exported calendar for the structure, and treat the breaks from June to
    October as best-case. Municipal colleagues on other rotations can find theirs on the
    <a href="/firefighter-shift-calendar">firefighter calendar hub</a> — including
    <a href="/houston-fire-department-shift-calendar">Houston</a> and
    <a href="/la-county-fire-shift-calendar">LA County</a> pre-anchored to their official calendars.
  </p>
</ToolPage>
